1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,175
In the last video,

2
00:00:02,175 --> 00:00:04,080
we defined escalations and

3
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:07,020
their benefits and went
over when to execute them.

4
00:00:07,020 --> 00:00:09,480
In this video, we're
going to cover how

5
00:00:09,480 --> 00:00:12,195
to communicate change
to your project team.

6
00:00:12,195 --> 00:00:14,640
You've probably come to
an understanding that

7
00:00:14,640 --> 00:00:16,320
effective communication leads to

8
00:00:16,320 --> 00:00:19,150
positive outcomes in every
aspect of your life.

9
00:00:19,150 --> 00:00:22,705
The same is true for communicating
changes in projects.

10
00:00:22,705 --> 00:00:24,290
Communicating change to

11
00:00:24,290 --> 00:00:26,014
project team members
and stakeholders

12
00:00:26,014 --> 00:00:27,230
isn't just a matter of

13
00:00:27,230 --> 00:00:29,555
keeping your tracking
documents up to date.

14
00:00:29,555 --> 00:00:32,000
Even the smallest changes
will be meaningful to

15
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:34,775
someone on your team and
should be communicated.

16
00:00:34,775 --> 00:00:36,860
It's also true that
you'll need to tailor

17
00:00:36,860 --> 00:00:38,720
your communication
tactics based on

18
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:40,700
the subject matter and recipient.

19
00:00:40,700 --> 00:00:42,065
As a project manager,

20
00:00:42,065 --> 00:00:43,490
sometimes you're
going to want to have

21
00:00:43,490 --> 00:00:44,960
a meeting with your whole team,

22
00:00:44,960 --> 00:00:48,005
while other times an
email might suffice.

23
00:00:48,005 --> 00:00:49,790
Personally, I prefer having

24
00:00:49,790 --> 00:00:51,380
a quick coffee or
hallway chat with

25
00:00:51,380 --> 00:00:53,270
my teammates and then
following up with

26
00:00:53,270 --> 00:00:55,760
an email, noting what
we agreed upon.

27
00:00:55,760 --> 00:00:58,190
This is particularly
useful when you need

28
00:00:58,190 --> 00:01:01,265
quick agreement or if the
subject is a little sensitive.

29
00:01:01,265 --> 00:01:02,240
How will you know

30
00:01:02,240 --> 00:01:04,550
which communication
tactic is appropriate?

31
00:01:04,550 --> 00:01:07,535
Ultimately, you'll have
to use your own judgment,

32
00:01:07,535 --> 00:01:08,810
but here are some ways you can

33
00:01:08,810 --> 00:01:10,445
decide which route to take.

34
00:01:10,445 --> 00:01:11,840
As a project manager,

35
00:01:11,840 --> 00:01:13,520
when communicating a small change

36
00:01:13,520 --> 00:01:15,094
that will affect an individual,

37
00:01:15,094 --> 00:01:17,420
it's a good idea
to send an email.

38
00:01:17,420 --> 00:01:19,835
Be sure to avoid emotional topics

39
00:01:19,835 --> 00:01:22,370
or anything that needs to
be discussed in depth.

40
00:01:22,370 --> 00:01:25,130
Just give them a heads up
and set a meeting time.

41
00:01:25,130 --> 00:01:27,365
Weekly meetings may
not be necessary,

42
00:01:27,365 --> 00:01:29,690
particularly if your
agenda is short.

43
00:01:29,690 --> 00:01:32,405
If you set a meeting and
then decide against it,

44
00:01:32,405 --> 00:01:34,250
you can either pivot
to an email or

45
00:01:34,250 --> 00:01:36,295
move the topic to
a different forum.

46
00:01:36,295 --> 00:01:37,535
When there's a big change

47
00:01:37,535 --> 00:01:39,110
within your project that impacts

48
00:01:39,110 --> 00:01:42,410
more than one person and is
likely to change the budget,

49
00:01:42,410 --> 00:01:44,310
deadline, or scope
of the project,

50
00:01:44,310 --> 00:01:46,760
you'll probably want to
have a team meeting.

51
00:01:46,760 --> 00:01:49,850
One useful tactic to keep
in mind when navigating

52
00:01:49,850 --> 00:01:53,000
these changes in your
project is called a timeout.

53
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:55,100
A timeout means taking a moment

54
00:01:55,100 --> 00:01:57,620
away from the project in
order to take a breath,

55
00:01:57,620 --> 00:02:00,355
regroup, and adjust
the game plan.

56
00:02:00,355 --> 00:02:03,710
A timeout may temporarily
disrupt your momentum,

57
00:02:03,710 --> 00:02:05,810
but it may be
absolutely necessary to

58
00:02:05,810 --> 00:02:08,375
set you up for success
in the long run.

59
00:02:08,375 --> 00:02:11,030
You may want to take a
timeout when the client

60
00:02:11,030 --> 00:02:13,520
wants to redefine the
scope of the project

61
00:02:13,520 --> 00:02:15,770
or if team members
get reassigned to

62
00:02:15,770 --> 00:02:19,085
other projects and you need
a plan to backfill them.

63
00:02:19,085 --> 00:02:21,950
This timeout is a chance
for the project team to

64
00:02:21,950 --> 00:02:23,630
evaluate the changes so they can

65
00:02:23,630 --> 00:02:25,750
adjust the plan as needed.

66
00:02:25,750 --> 00:02:27,800
Throughout the process,
you will want to hold

67
00:02:27,800 --> 00:02:30,095
meetings in order to
discuss successes,

68
00:02:30,095 --> 00:02:32,060
setbacks, and possible future

69
00:02:32,060 --> 00:02:34,070
improvements to the project.

70
00:02:34,070 --> 00:02:37,220
These meetings are
called retrospectives.

71
00:02:37,220 --> 00:02:38,870
Retrospectives are held

72
00:02:38,870 --> 00:02:40,690
throughout the
project's life cycle.

73
00:02:40,690 --> 00:02:42,665
A retrospective focuses on

74
00:02:42,665 --> 00:02:44,750
identifying the
contributing causes of

75
00:02:44,750 --> 00:02:46,370
an incident or pattern of

76
00:02:46,370 --> 00:02:49,330
incidents without
blaming one individual.

77
00:02:49,330 --> 00:02:51,470
While conducting a retrospective,

78
00:02:51,470 --> 00:02:53,630
you should always assume
that everyone has

79
00:02:53,630 --> 00:02:54,950
good intentions and did

80
00:02:54,950 --> 00:02:57,215
the right thing with the
information they had,

81
00:02:57,215 --> 00:02:59,870
whether or not it worked
out well in the end.

82
00:02:59,870 --> 00:03:03,085
There's always a chance
to learn and do better.

83
00:03:03,085 --> 00:03:04,625
As a project manager,

84
00:03:04,625 --> 00:03:05,960
it's important to identify

85
00:03:05,960 --> 00:03:07,850
effective techniques
for communicating

86
00:03:07,850 --> 00:03:11,440
changes to an individual
teammate or to your whole team.

87
00:03:11,440 --> 00:03:13,370
You now know a bit
more about taking

88
00:03:13,370 --> 00:03:15,679
a timeout and the idea
of a retrospective

89
00:03:15,679 --> 00:03:17,525
to have productive conversations

90
00:03:17,525 --> 00:03:20,620
about incidents that arise
during your project,

91
00:03:20,620 --> 00:03:24,275
and we will discuss this more
towards the end of the course.

92
00:03:24,275 --> 00:03:25,760
All of these things will help

93
00:03:25,760 --> 00:03:26,900
you on your journey to become

94
00:03:26,900 --> 00:03:30,780
a successful project
manager. Let's keep going.
